BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ideas to Strengthen Democracy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T193000
UID:6ac676ecde300e004518d694@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:On the heels of the municipal election\, this conversation wil
 l explore the small steps and giant leaps we could take to make democracy 
 more welcoming\, accessible\, and sustainable for everyone. As public tr
 ust\, civic participation\, and expectations of government continue to evo
 lve\, Canadians are asking important questions about how to strengthen dem
 ocratic engagement\, lower barriers to participation\, and create healthie
 r conditions for public service.\nThis session will bring together people 
 who have served in elected office and government\nModerator and panelists\
 nleadership alongside leading thinkers in democratic innovation and public
  policy. Drawing on their diverse experiences\, they will examine how we c
 an make democracy work better both for the public and for those who choose
  to serve within it. The discussion will explore how to create more constr
 uctive public dialogue\, encourage broader participation in civic life\, s
 upport current and future leaders\, and strengthen the institutions that u
 nderpin democratic governance.\nFrom rebuilding civic trust and fostering 
 respectful debate to reimagining pathways into public service and revisiti
 ng conversations about electoral reform\, panelists will share practical i
 deas for renewing democracy and ensuring it is fit for the challenges and 
 opportunities of the future.\nConversation to be followed by a reception f
 rom 7:30-8:30 pm.\nFeaturing:\nDominique Anglade\n\, former Deputy Premier
  of Quebec\, former leader of the Quebec Liberal Party\, and business comm
 unity leader\;\nThe Honourable Christine Elliott\n\, former Deputy Premier
  of Ontario\, former Minister of Health of Ontario\, and longtime public s
 ervant with decades of experience in provincial politics and public policy
 \;\nPeter MacLeod\n\, founder of MASS LBP and co-author of\nDemocracy's Se
 cond Act: Why Politics Needs the Public\n\;\nSean Speer\n\, senior fellow 
 at the University of Toronto's Munk School of Global Affairs and Public Po
 licy\, fellow in strategic competitiveness at the Public Policy Forum\, an
 d Editor-at-large at The Hub\nModerated by:\nJennifer Hollett\, Executive 
 Director\, The Walrus\nToronto's municipal election takes place on October
  26\, 2026. Check out the\nKnowYourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebrate De
 mocracy\npage to learn more about the upcoming election\, access important
  voter resources\, and discover more activities and interactive library pr
 ograms for all ages happening at a branch near you.\nThis initiative is ge
 nerously supported by the McConnell Foundation and Max Bell Foundation.\nI
 nformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is co
 llected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sec
 tions 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for eve
 nt and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how
  the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff 
 at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W
  2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac676ecde300e004518d694
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Get Things Done with AI: Build Your Own Productivity System
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T200000
UID:6a9b4e391c197d11325d6f21@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What if your to-do list could check in on you\, notice what's 
 been stuck for weeks\, and offer to help instead of just sitting there?\nI
 n this class led by our Innovator-in-Residence\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, you'll ex
 plore a personal productivity system built around an AI agent and a weekly
 -review practice. See how the agent loads goals\, counts what actually got
  done\, flags the items that keep slipping\, and drafts next week's plan. 
 It can support without cheerleading\, asking "Why has this been stuck for 
 three weeks? Here are your options." You'll try it yourself with a practic
 e set of goals\, and leave with the plain-text templates and a clearer map
  of how an AI might support you\, ready to build your own system at home.\
 nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. Computers are p
 rovided. No coding experience required.\nRegistration is required and wil
 l start September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaran
 teed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remai
 ning seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until capaci
 ty is reached. If you wish to save your work from this hands-on program\,
  please bring your own USB stick.\nAbout the AI Tools Used in This Program
 \nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI tools (listed below
 ) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your own account for this
  program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Resident’s guidance
  throughout sessions\, which include best practices on using AI.\nWe encou
 rage you to review the privacy statements of each tool prior to attending 
 this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy poli
 cy\nClaude Cowork\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nGoogl
 e Antigravity\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nGoogle AI
  Studio\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e391c197d11325d6f21
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
