BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Discussing Treaties with Dr. Rick Monture
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T193000
UID:6ad63569ebad220045465c5f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join Dr. Rick Monture for a discussion about pre-contact peace
  agreements between Indigenous nations\, treaties between Indigenous peopl
 es and Europeans\, and the various "numbered" treaties in Canada. Learn ab
 out the philosophies behind the Dish With One Spoon wampum and its signifi
 cance today\, as well as the 1784 Haldimand Proclamation as it pertains to
  First Nations in Southern Ontario. Come discover why treaties are a vita
 l part of the movement towards Truth and Reconciliation!\nNo registration 
 required.\nBio:\nRick Monture is a member of the Mohawk nation\, Turtle cl
 an\, from Six Nations of the Grand River Territory.  He is a Professor in 
 the Department of English & Cultural Studies and Indigenous Studies at McM
 aster University where he teaches classes on Indigenous literature\, Haude
 nosaunee history\, traditional narratives\, and Bob Dylan.  He is also a C
 ommunity Research Fellow with Six Nations Polytechnic and the Deyohahage /
  Indigenous Knowledge Centre based at Six Nations and is an active member 
 of the language revitalization movement in his community.  In partnership 
 with the Woodland Cultural Centre in Brantford\, Rick currently holds a So
 cial Science and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC) award for a
  project that examines the events that led to the overthrow of the traditi
 onal Haudenosaunee government\, by Ottawa\, to implement an elected counci
 l system in 1924.  His book\,\nWe Share our Matters: Two Centuries of Writ
 ing and Resistance at Six Nations of the Grand River\n\, was published in 
 2014 and is an examination of the Grand River Haudenosaunee’s assertions
  of sovereignty and social justice through letters\, political activism\, 
 literature\, and film. Rick lives at Six Nations with his family.
LOCATION:Spadina Road
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad63569ebad220045465c5f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
